I notice a potential issue with operator precedence in the curl detection. Let me verify:
  ╭─ Turn 28 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  ▶ read src/safety.rs:1043..1053 ✓ (1ms)

Found a real bug! On line 1044-1046, there's an operator precedence issue. In Rust, && has higher precedence than ||, so this evaluates as:

```
contains("--upload-file") || (contains("-t ") && (contains("ftp://") || contains("sftp://")))
```

This means --upload-file triggers without checking for ftp/sftp, which is actually the intended behavior since --upload-file is always exfiltration. But the -t  check should be -T  (uppercase T) since curl -T is the upload flag, and -t is for TLS-SRP. Let me verify:
  ╭─ Turn 29 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  ▶ web search 'curl -T flag --upload-file equivalent' ✓ (903ms)

Confirmed! curl -T (uppercase T) is the upload flag. The code uses lowercase -t  which is actually --tls-max or a telnet option, not the upload flag. This is a real bug.

